      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As soil pH increases above 7.0, the chemical equilibrium shifts dramatically, causing micronutrient insolubility. In alkaline conditions, the free metal ions ($\\text{Fe}^{2+}$, $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$) readily react with hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) present in the soil solution. This reaction precipitates the elements into highly insoluble hydroxides ($\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$ or $\\text{MnO}_2$), which are unavailable for passive or active root absorption. Essentially, the elevated $\\text{OH}^-$ concentration drives the metal ions out of solution and into solid, immobile forms.",
      "source": "target",
